        <div id="cycles-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Battery Chemistry, Cycle Ratings and Warranty
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every current Jackery and EcoFlow model in this comparison uses LiFePO4, lithium iron phosphate, cells. That is a meaningful change from a few years ago, when Jackery's original Explorer line used NMC lithium-ion rated for roughly 500 cycles. LiFePO4 trades a little energy density for far longer cycle life and better thermal stability, and our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion power stations</a> covers why that trade is worth making for anything you plan to keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cycle numbers themselves need reading carefully, because the two brands rate to different end points. Jackery typically quotes cycles to 70 percent of original capacity: 4,000 cycles to 70 percent on the Explorer 1000 v2, 2000 v2 and 5000 Plus. EcoFlow quotes cycles to 80 percent: 4,000 cycles to 80 percent on the DELTA 3, DELTA 3 Plus and DELTA Pro 3. Reaching 80 percent is a stricter standard than reaching 70 percent, so a 4,000-cycle EcoFlow rating is, on its face, the stronger claim of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jackery's newer HomePower v2 line moves the goalposts the other way, with 6,000-plus cycles quoted for the HomePower 2000 Plus v2. In practice, all of these ratings are far beyond what most owners use. Even daily full cycling, which almost no backup or camping owner does, would take more than ten years to reach 4,000 cycles. For a typical owner the battery will outlast the electronics around it, and the cycle figure is a tiebreaker, not a deciding factor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty is where the fine print matters more. Jackery lists most current models as 3 plus 2 years, meaning a three-year standard warranty with two extra years when you buy from Jackery directly or an authorised seller. EcoFlow lists five years on its current DELTA models, which on several product pages is described as a three-year standard warranty plus two years on registration in the EcoFlow app. Either way, register the unit the day it arrives and keep the receipt, because the extended years are not automatic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging-solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Wall Charging Speed and Solar Input
          </h2>

          <p>
            Charging speed is EcoFlow's strongest category. Its X-Stream charging pulls 1500W from a wall outlet on the DELTA 3 Plus, refilling it in 56 minutes, and 1800W on the 2 kWh DELTA 3 Max models, reaching 80 percent in 64 to 68 minutes. Jackery's standard charging is slower, 1.58 hours on the Explorer 1000 v2 and 1.75 hours on the Explorer 2000 v2, though both have an emergency fast-charge mode, switched on in the app, that brings the 1000 v2 down to about an hour and the 2000 v2 to about 1.3 hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why that matters: if a storm is forecast and your unit is at 30 percent, you can top up an EcoFlow in the gap between the warning and the outage. In a long outage with a generator, faster charging also means less generator run time per refill. For camping or occasional use, where the unit sits on a charger overnight, the difference is close to irrelevant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solar input is the bigger practical gap. At 1 kWh, EcoFlow accepts 1000W of solar across two inputs against 400W on the Explorer 1000 v2. At 2 kWh, the DELTA 3 Max Plus takes 1000W against 800W on the HomePower 2000 Plus v2, and the non-expandable models take 500W and 400W respectively. If you plan to run off solar for days at a time, that ceiling decides how many panels you can usefully connect and how fast a short sunny window refills the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands use their own connectors on their panels but accept standard panels within their voltage windows, which run roughly 11 to 60 volts on the smaller units. Our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> guide covers matching panels to these inputs, and if you would rather buy a matched kit, our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">best solar generator</a> roundup compares the bundles directly. Remember that a panel's rated wattage is a lab figure; in real sun expect roughly 60 to 80 percent of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ups-app-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. UPS Switchover, App and Noise
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands sell their units as uninterruptible power supplies, meaning devices stay plugged into the station, the station stays plugged into the wall, and it takes over when the grid drops. The figure to compare is switchover time. EcoFlow lists under 10 ms across the DELTA 3 family and 10 ms on the DELTA Pro 3. Jackery lists under 20 ms on the Explorer 1000 v2 and the Explorer 5000 Plus in backup mode, under 10 ms on the HomePower 1000 Plus v2, and 0 ms in the 5000 Plus's online mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most desktop computers and network gear ride through 20 ms without noticing, but some sensitive equipment, older desktop power supplies and some aquarium or medical controllers can reset on a longer gap. If the reason you are buying is to keep a desktop PC or a home server running through flickers, the faster switchover is worth paying attention to, and the EcoFlow DELTA 3 models or Jackery's newer HomePower v2 units are the safer bets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both apps connect over Wi-Fi and Bluetooth and let you monitor input and output, toggle outlets, and set charge limits. EcoFlow's app is the more ambitious of the two, with time-of-use charging and a storm warning mode that charges the unit automatically when severe weather is forecast. Jackery's app is simpler and gates its emergency fast charge mode, which is worth knowing because you need the app to get the advertised charge times.
          </p>

          <p>
            On noise, both brands now quote very quiet figures under light loads: EcoFlow under 30 dB at up to 600W on the DELTA 3 and 25 dB at 600W on the DELTA 3 Max models, and Jackery 30 dB in its quiet mode. Under heavy load or fast charging, the cooling fans on any of these units become clearly audible, so if it will live in a bedroom, plan to charge it during the day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy EcoFlow if your priority is home backup. Its higher inverter output at every size below the whole-home class, its faster wall charging, its larger solar input and its sub-10 ms UPS switchover all point the same way: it is the better machine for keeping a house running. The DELTA 3 Plus is the sweet spot for most households, and the DELTA 3 Max Plus is the one to choose if you need to start a well pump or run a window air conditioner alongside a fridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Jackery if you value low weight and simplicity, particularly for camping, RV use or a first power station that someone else in the household also needs to operate. The Explorer 1000 v2, which Jackery bills as the smallest and lightest 1 kWh LiFePO4 unit on the market, has the simplest controls here, and the HomePower 2000 Plus v2 makes a 2 kWh expandable system that one person can actually carry. At the very top end, the Explorer 5000 Plus is the pick if you need 7200W of 240V output and do not need to move it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are torn, decide by the one number that matters most to you. Need more than 1500W from a 1 kWh unit? EcoFlow. Need it under 25 pounds? Jackery. Need to refill quickly from the wall before a storm? EcoFlow. Need the most raw 240V output for the money? Jackery's 5000 Plus. Most buyers find one of those questions settles it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever brand you lean toward, check how the unit compares to the other LiFePO4 options from Bluetti and Anker before you buy. All of them, with our testing notes and the specific use each suits best, are in our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a>, and if a fridge is the main load you are planning around, our guide to whether a <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">portable power station can run a refrigerator</a> works through the runtimes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing cycle ratings at face value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery usually rates cycles to 70 percent capacity and EcoFlow to 80 percent, so 4,000 cycles does not mean the same thing on both spec sheets. Compare the end point as well as the number, and remember that most owners never come close to either limit in the life of the unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming advertised charge times are automatic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery's fastest wall-charging figures require switching on emergency charging in the app; the standard rate is slower. EcoFlow's quickest times assume optimal conditions too. If fast charging before a storm matters to you, learn how to switch the mode on before you actually need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 1500W when you need 1800W</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The gap between the Explorer 1000 v2 and DELTA 3 Plus outputs sounds small but decides whether a 1500W heater, microwave or kettle runs comfortably. Add up the wattage of what you will actually plug in, including startup surges, before choosing a unit on weight or price alone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to register for the extended warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands advertise five years, but on many models two of those years depend on buying through the right channel or registering in the app. Register the day the unit arrives and keep the receipt, or you may be on three years without knowing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring solar input limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Connecting more panel wattage than the unit accepts does nothing for charge speed. The Explorer 1000 v2 stops at 400W, the DELTA 3 Plus at 1000W. Size your solar array to the unit's input ceiling and voltage window rather than buying panels first and hoping they fit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

