        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Jackery or EcoFlow better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home backup buyers, EcoFlow, because its current models offer more AC output, faster wall charging, higher solar input and faster UPS switchover at each size up to 2 kWh. Jackery is better if weight and simplicity matter most, and its Explorer 5000 Plus out-powers EcoFlow's DELTA Pro 3 at the whole-home end.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better, Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 or EcoFlow DELTA 3?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The DELTA 3 Plus is the more capable unit, with 1800W of output against 1500W, six AC outlets against three, 1000W of solar input against 400W, and expansion to 5 kWh. The Explorer 1000 v2 is 3.8 pounds lighter and simpler to operate. Choose the EcoFlow for home backup and the Jackery for portability.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Jackery and EcoFlow both use LiFePO4 batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, across their current main lineups. Every model in this comparison uses LiFePO4 cells. Older Jackery Explorer models used NMC lithium-ion with much lower cycle ratings, so if you are buying used or on clearance, check the spec sheet for the cell chemistry before assuming the long cycle life applies.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which charges faster, Jackery or EcoFlow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                EcoFlow, in standard mode. The DELTA 3 Plus refills in 56 minutes from a wall outlet and the DELTA 3 Max models reach 80 percent in about an hour. Jackery's Explorer 1000 v2 takes 1.58 hours normally and about an hour in its app-enabled emergency mode, so the gap narrows once you switch that on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix Jackery and EcoFlow batteries or panels?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expansion batteries are brand and model specific, so you cannot add an EcoFlow battery to a Jackery or vice versa. Solar panels are more flexible: either brand accepts third-party panels within its voltage and wattage limits, sometimes with an adapter cable for the connector. Check the input voltage window before connecting any panel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which has the better warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On paper they are close. Jackery lists most current models at three years plus two extra when bought directly or through authorised sellers. EcoFlow lists five years on its current DELTA models, described on several pages as three years plus two on app registration. In both cases, register promptly to secure the full term.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the EcoFlow DELTA 2 still worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you find one discounted, yes. It has 1024 Wh of LiFePO4 capacity, 1800W of output and expansion, and it remains a capable unit. EcoFlow's US store now steers buyers to the DELTA 3, which adds faster charging and a 4,000-cycle rating, so compare prices before choosing the older model.
              </p>
            </div>

